WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.080
Quantization lowers numerical precision so a model uses less memory and often runs more

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.500
practically on local hardware.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.995
Labels such as Q2 through Q8 describe broad precision levels,

00:00:09.995 --> 00:00:12.000
but exact schemes and model behavior differ.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.190
Lower-bit quantization generally reduces file and memory size while increasing the chance

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:17.000
of quality loss.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.200
Higher precision uses more resources and is not automatically better for every task,

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.067
runtime, or machine. Test the actual file.

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:29.530
G G U F is a common local model format for llama.cpp-compatible runtimes.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:32.950
Compatibility depends on architecture, runtime version, tokenizer, prompt template,

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:36.460
metadata, and acceleration backend, not only the file extension.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:40.500
Memory fit includes model weights, key-value cache, context, batch settings,

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.500
concurrent sessions, runtime overhead, display use, and other applications.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.985
File size is therefore not a complete VRAM or unified-memory estimate.

00:00:47.985 --> 00:00:51.160
Some runtimes allow different cache precision or memory strategies,

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.500
creating another quality, compatibility, and capacity tradeoff.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.170
Treat those settings as part of the tested configuration instead of assuming model

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.195
quantization tells the whole story.

00:01:00.195 --> 00:01:04.500
Partial GPU offload can run some layers on the accelerator and leave others on the CPU.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.500
This may make a larger model load, but transfer and CPU work can reduce speed.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.500
A technically successful load is not an operational success.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.590
Avoid configurations that consume nearly all available memory, swap heavily,

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:18.492
crash on long prompts, or throttle under sustained work.

00:01:18.492 --> 00:01:21.520
Compare several quantizations and sizes with realistic context,

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.500
then preserve a margin for stability.
